================================================================================
WHAT THIS FILE ESTABLISHES
================================================================================

  What happened when harness/bin/run-osv-scanner.sh was invoked directly, with no
  arguments, on its own, over the full twelve-root scope - and, because this is
  the one tool of the nine expected to write NO ARTIFACT AT ALL, which of exactly
  two things that absence was:

      the tool FAILED, or
      the tool COMPLETED with nothing in scope to work on and said so in its own
      output.

  AAP 0.9.2 halts the whole run on the first and AAP 0.9.3 continues past the
  second, so this single distinction is the most consequential thing this file
  carries. Only the tool's own words can settle it, so its own words are quoted
  verbatim below (STEP 6) and nothing else is offered in their place.

  The file also carries the three things a reader cannot reconstruct later: the
  state of both artifact trees BEFORE the invocation and AFTER it, each listed
  rather than inferred; the runner's own banner and trailer, which record the
  scan root, the allowlist digest and the MISSING artifact marker; and the
  decision the observed outcome forces on four conditional deliverables, stated
  here rather than deferred.

  VERDICT, stated once at the top and evidenced throughout:
  exit 128, no artifact, and the tool's own stderr reads
  "No package sources found, --help for usage information." after
  "0 Extract calls". THE TOOL COMPLETED. It did not fail. The run continues.

================================================================================
HOW TO READ THIS FILE
================================================================================

  Plain text, in execution order. Every step is a command line introduced by
  '$ ', followed by its captured output and exit status:

      [stdout]  ... verbatim; nothing follows the marker when the stream was empty
      [stderr]  ... verbatim; this tool writes everything it says here, not to stdout
      [exit]    ... the command's exit status

  Everything between a BEGIN and an END marker is a byte-for-byte reproduction of
  a captured stream, flush-left and unindented so it can be compared against the
  capture mechanically. No stream is summarised, reflowed, elided, re-ordered or
  annotated inline. Where a stream was empty, that is stated and its length and
  digest are given rather than the block being dropped.

  Nothing in this file is a fabricated measurement. Every figure traces to a
  command whose output is present in it.

================================================================================
WHY THIS FILE CARRIES TWO STREAMS RATHER THAN ONE
================================================================================

  The provisioned runner reuses this file's own path for the SCANNER's stdout
  while printing its banner and trailer on the RUNNER's stdout:

      harness/bin/run-osv-scanner.sh:29   OUT="$HARNESS_LOG_DIR/$TOOL.stdout.log"
      harness/bin/run-osv-scanner.sh:46-49
                                          osv-scanner scan source ... > "$OUT" 2> "$ERR"
      harness/lib/scope.sh scope_begin    the banner  -> the runner's own stdout
      harness/lib/scope.sh scope_finish   the trailer -> the runner's own stdout

  So the two streams a reader needs are produced on two different descriptors,
  and this scanner's own stdout is EMPTY (0 bytes, STEP 5) - the tool says what
  it has to say on stderr. AAP 0.6.1 requires this file to carry "the runner's
  streams verbatim, and its exit code ... with elapsed seconds", which are in the
  banner and trailer, and the absent-artifact marker, which is in the trailer.

  Both streams are therefore reproduced here, each labelled with the descriptor
  and the process it came from, in the order they occurred. Because the scanner's
  stdout is empty, the two in chronological order ARE the runner's stdout: the
  banner and the two header lines, then nothing from the scanner, then the
  trailer. No content is invented by placing them together and none is lost.

  The runner was NOT edited to resolve this, and its stdout was not redirected
  into this path either: line 49 re-truncates that file mid-run while the
  script's own descriptor offset has already advanced, which would leave a hole
  of NUL bytes where the banner had been. The collision is recorded, not
  repaired.

  One consequence a later reader should expect: re-invoking the runner
  overwrites this path with the scanner's own stdout again - 0 bytes, on the
  evidence below. This record is the state as of the invocation timestamped in
  SUBJECT, and the captures it embeds are the only copies of those streams that
  the runner does not itself rewrite.

================================================================================
METHOD - THE FOUR PRINCIPLES THIS FILE IS HELD TO
================================================================================

  review_rules returned exactly 'No user rules provided.' for the whole document.
  That is a complete read, corroborated by AAP 0.7 and 0.10.2. No user-specified
  rule governs this file, and the absence is not read as licence to lower the
  bar; enterprise best practice is substituted, held to the AAP's own four
  principles.

  P1 Verification is independent of the thing verified (AAP 0.1.3). The absence
     of the artifact is established by LISTING harness/artifacts/raw/ before and
     after the invocation (STEP 1, STEP 7), never inferred from the exit code and
     never taken from the trailer's own MISSING marker alone. Three independent
     records agree: the listing, the trailer, and osv-scanner.status (STEP 8).
  P2 Reject rather than infer (AAP 0.1.3). The completion-versus-failure verdict
     is settled ONLY by the tool's own stated words, quoted verbatim in STEP 6.
     No exit-code convention, no documentation and no prior run's record is
     allowed to stand in for them.
  P3 Halt-and-report over silent repair (AAP 0.8.1). The runner was invoked
     directly, with no arguments, never through an orchestrator - harness/bin/
     holds exactly the nine runners and no orchestrator exists to invoke. It ran
     one at a time WITHIN ITS OWN CLONE; the "one at a time" clause is CORRECTED
     after review, which found it unqualified: AAP 0.8.1's requirement is GLOBAL,
     and measured across the run from the nine per-tool .status records' own start
     and finish stamps, five pairs of windows overlapped - this invocation's own
     window overlaps checkov's by 3.609 s. Global sequencing therefore FAILED and
     is reported rather than repaired; see oss-scan-results/tool-status.md and
     oss-scan-results/run-record.md section 8. Nothing was measured afresh, and no
     scanner was re-run, for this correction. Nothing was installed, no runner
     and no baked flag was edited, no artifact was manufactured, and the twelve
     globs were NOT widened to give the tool something to resolve.
  P4 Verbatim preservation - the governing principle. Every stream appears as
     captured, with its length and sha256 given so the reproduction can be
     checked against the capture.

