================================================================================
VERDICT - WHICH OF THE TWO CASES THIS WAS
================================================================================

  THE TOOL COMPLETED WITH NOTHING IN SCOPE TO WORK ON, AND SAID SO IN ITS OWN
  OUTPUT. IT DID NOT FAIL. THE RUN CONTINUES PAST IT.

  Settled on the tool's own words alone (STEP 6), which are the only admissible
  basis:

      "End status: 640 dirs visited, 4735 inodes visited, 0 Extract calls,
       2.103098503s elapsed, 2.103098903s wall time"
      "No package sources found, --help for usage information."

  Zero Extract calls after visiting 640 directories is the tool stating that it
  reached the scope and found no manifest or lockfile to extract packages from.
  This is long-standing documented behaviour for a zero-package input rather than
  a crash (osv-scanner issue #348, and the same message reported for SBOMs
  without package URLs in issue #93, both cited in AAP 0.2.2).

  Under AAP 0.5.4 the artifact status for this tool is therefore

      artifact status : absent
      basis           : artifact absent AND the tool stated a no-work reason in
                        its own output; stderr quoted verbatim above
      rows            : zero
      run             : CONTINUES

  and the halting condition 'a tool writing no artifact with no stated reason of
  its own' (AAP 0.9.2) is NOT met. Exit 128 is recorded as a fact and is not
  itself the basis for the verdict: artifact status and exit status are
  independent (AAP 0.5.4), which is why two of the nine tools legitimately exit
  non-zero because they found something.

================================================================================
WHY NOTHING RESOLVED - THE ONE MANIFEST-SHAPED FILE IN SCOPE
================================================================================

  This is a property of the SCOPE, not of the installation. The tool is proven
  functional: it resolved its own version, walked all eighteen directories and
  reported its own end status.

  Exactly one manifest-shaped file exists in the twelve authoritative roots:

      core/src/main/resources/org/apache/spark/ui/static/package.json
      80 bytes, 5 lines
      sha256 43b4dcbf33dc23b3d62576dce22b371b1d0c852b05dfd73442e9c3e97a0b4717
      (measured in the pinned clone at /opt/spark-src)

      {
        "name": "spark-ui",
        "license": "Apache License 2.0",
        "type": "module"
      }

  A name, a license and a type. NO dependencies block, no package-lock.json
  beside it and no node_modules directory. So nothing in scope resolves to a
  package, and a scanner whose whole job is resolving packages has nothing to
  resolve. There is no pom.xml, no requirements*.txt, no setup.py, no
  pyproject.toml and no JAR anywhere in the eighteen directories.

  ZERO RESOLVABLE DEPENDENCY MANIFESTS IN SCOPE IS A FINDING TO REPORT WITH ITS
